import sys; sys.path.insert(0,'..')
from con_helpers import *
save(3,1,"Vṛtti: The Waves of the Mind","Once today, when a feeling arrives, say to yourself: a wave of this is here. Then name its kind and read its dials.",[
mc("U","What is a vṛtti, according to Conversation 3.1?",
 ["A wave-formation in the mind that rises, lasts a while and subsides, whatever its kind.","A fixed trait of character, which a person carries with them throughout life.","A rule of practice, which a teacher gives to a student at the start of study.","A state of deep calm, which comes only after long years of steady practice."],0,
 "The word comes from a root meaning to turn or whirl. Every thought, perception, memory and feeling, even dreamless sleep, is a wave-formation that rises, lasts a while and subsides.",
 "A vṛtti is any wave of the mind: it rises, lasts, subsides","Part 1"),
mc("A","A man tells himself, “I am anxious,” and feels stuck. What shift does Conversation 3.1 offer him?",
 ["Say a wave of anxiety is here, since a fact cannot change but a wave rises and falls.","Say I am not anxious at all, so that the feeling is pushed out of the mind.","Say I am anxious because of my past, so that the cause can be traced and cleared.","Say nothing about it, since naming a feeling is the surest way to make it grow."],0,
 "A fact cannot change, but a wave rises and falls. That single shift gives a suffering person a foothold: the difference between drowning and noticing you are in water.",
 "A wave of anxiety is here, not I am anxious","Part 1"),
judge("“In Vivekananda’s picture of the mind-lake, stilling the mind means forcing the waves flat.”",F,
 "Stilling the mind is letting the water settle and not forcing the waves flat. The still bottom is the deepest Self, the water is the mind and the waves are the vṛttis. A wave is only the water taking a shape, and it leaves no stain.",
 "Stilling is letting the water settle, not flattening the waves","Part 1"),
mc("U","What are the three dials of a wave in Conversation 3.1?",
 ["Size, speed and alignment, none of which is fixed, since each of them can be turned.","Height, colour and pitch, which are set at birth and which a person cannot change.","Past, present and future, which decide how long a wave goes on ringing in the mind.","Body, breath and mind, which together decide how fully a person is awake or asleep."],0,
 "Size (how big and vivid, set by the body and prāṇa), speed (how fast it races or drags, held by manas) and alignment (whether thoughts, feelings, body and intention pull together or scatter). The dials can be turned, which is the whole hope of the practices ahead.",
 "The three dials are size, speed and alignment, and they can be turned","Part 3"),
mc("A","Two patients are both called anxious. One is large, fast and scattered; the other small, slow and flat. What follows from Conversation 3.1?",
 ["Treat both the same, since one label means one kind of treatment for every person.","Treat the dials and not the label, since the same word can describe two different waves.","Treat the first only, since a flat wave is a sign that nothing is the matter at all.","Treat neither, since waves of this kind are bound to pass of their own accord."],1,
 "Anxious can be large-fast-scattered or small-slow-flat, so you treat the dials and not the label. Naming the label alone hides the difference that matters most.",
 "Treat the dials, not the label","Part 3"),
mc("A","A woman is sure she saw a snake on the path, and it was a rope. Which kind of wave is this, in Conversation 3.1?",
 ["Misperception: vivid but false, a wave that does not match what is really there.","Clear knowing: the wave matches what is real, so she was right to be afraid.","Imagination: there is no outside object, so it was a poem more than a fear.","Memory: the wave repainted an old event, coloured by her mood on that day."],0,
 "The five kinds are clear knowing, misperception, imagination, sleep and memory. The rope seen as a snake is the classic misperception: vivid, but false.",
 "A rope seen as a snake is misperception","Part 4"),
mc("A","A man keeps replaying a painful memory. What does Conversation 3.1 suggest about it?",
 ["Memory is a playback, so it can never be changed by anything that he does now.","Memory is repainted in the present, so it can be repainted more gently over time.","Memory is imagination, so he should simply decide that it never took place.","Memory is a misperception, so he should argue with it until it goes away."],1,
 "Memory is not playback but a present-tense repainting, coloured by today’s mood, so a painful memory can be repainted more gently.",
 "Memory is repainted in the present, so it can soften","Part 4"),
judge("“Naming the kind of wave and reading its dials puts you in the position of the one noticing it, and not of the one lost in it.”",T,
 "You do not argue a wave away. You name what kind it is and read its dials, such as a worry-wave, large and fast, and at once you are the one noticing it. Suffering becomes a pattern, and patterns can change.",
 "Naming the kind of wave is the first freedom","Part 4"),
mc("A","A doctor wants to ask a patient a better first question than “what is wrong with me?” Which fits Conversation 3.1?",
 ["What wave is here, of which kind, how big, and is it accurate to what is really there?","What went wrong in your childhood, and which event first set this pattern in motion?","What would a stronger person do in your place, and why can you not do the same?","What have you been told is the cause, and do you agree with the people who said so?"],0,
 "In the clinic, the question moves from what is wrong with me to what wave is here, which kind, how big, and is it accurate? Suffering becomes a pattern, and patterns can change.",
 "Ask what wave is here, its kind and its dials","Part 4"),
judge("“Every experience can be heard in two ways, as one flowing sound in time or as a whole pattern all at once, and both are complete.”",T,
 "Like a musical chord, an experience can be heard moment after moment, as we normally live, or as its whole pattern of notes at once and timeless. Both are complete.",
 "A chord can be heard in time or all at once, and both are complete","Part 2")])
